								<div class="title" id="post-title-4221142">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200837" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1555445969 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Apr 2019 20:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4221142">
									

<p>Mhm. This is&#8230; an odd one. I really like ghost stories, and things that play with that concept in the clinical setting of the Foundation, but this&#8230; I'm not sure. It's a fairly standard ghost premise (not that that's a bad thing!), but the entire story is essentially conveyed through expository dialogue, and the characters aren't really engaging or unique enough for that to pay off on its own. I was excited to hear about how the wounds came about &#8212; they're <em>weird</em> &#8212; but the ending felt like a let down after the buildup relating to the Foundation's internal management of ghosts, which in itself is something I'd be really interested to read about.</p>
<p>You start off with strong apparition imagery, but it gets bogged down with straightforward &quot;here's what happened&quot; interviews, a fairly irrelevant tidbit about &quot;<em>Apparition Escalation: Preliminary Research into Human Triggered Escalation of Apparition Hostility&quot;</em> &#8212; which, again, I'd love to be a more major part of the article &#8212; and characters that just seem&#8230; flat.</p>
<p>Sorry, man; gonna have to novote this one. It's not <em>bad</em>, but the story being told here is pretty much the most straightforward ghost story you could come up with. Given the imagery you came up with for stuff like 4099, I'm certain you've got it in you to do the concept justice.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Grammar/wording&nbsp;nitpicks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Should SCP-4435 begin exhibiting unnatural behavior, the chamber is to be kept at a high setting to prevent SCP-4435 from dematerializing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;kept&quot; feels like a weird word choice here. I feel like it should be &quot;set at&quot; or &quot;turned to&quot; or something; 'kept' implies a permanence that goes against the &quot;unnatural behaviour&quot; line.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Specifically, the Coracohumeral and Capsular ligaments of the shoulder, the Palmar Radiocarpal ligaments in the wrists, and the lateral collateral ligaments and the Patelllar tendons near the Cruciate ligaments for the knee joints.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You don't need to capitalise these terms.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it has shown to fully change to either one of these states</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you're missing a word in this clause.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4435 was discovered by PoI-9009 (&quot;Kevin Nguyen&quot;) after the subject had reported a &quot;supernatural presence&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This works without the word &quot;had&quot;, and its inclusion feels weird to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Under no circumstances are D-9009 and SCP-4435 be in the same room together.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, a missing word.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

